+*PRIVACY TEAM REVIEW*+ 

 

{panel:bgColor=#deebff}
*PROJECT KODAK - CCTV PARTNERSHIP*
{panel}

As laid down by [~accountid:63fe21787c30bbd6b33e09d3], there are several potential privacy issues that need to be reviewed. I will break down each part below - let me know if I misunderstood something.

----

{panel:bgColor=#fffae6}
*_Data sharing_*
{panel}

What would help our position greatly and reduce privacy risks is having a guide / policy of when we receive the recordings.

In other words, if we can defend that 1) the cameras are completely optional, 2) CCTV monitoring is done only when chosen by the drivers and 3) Driver shares recordings only when he/she believes it’s necessary for safety reasons, I believe we will be minimizing any associated privacy risks.

To sum up, what would minimize the risk is if we establish that *Bolt will not have routine access to CCTV footage*, reinforcing the separation of data control.

{panel:bgColor=#fffae6}
*_Legal basis and informing the riders_*
{panel}

To be able to install CCTV cameras in their cars, the Drivers will have to inform the Riders about the fact that CCTV is in action. Consent would most likely not be needed in this case, as recording would be done based on another legal basis - legitimate interest.

Even though Bolt is not responsible for the CCTV cameras, we should at least nudge the Drivers to have some sort of stickers in their car that would be in a visible place and would properly inform the Riders that they are under surveillance. From practical perspective, I believe this would reduce the amount of complaints we receive from Riders.

{panel:bgColor=#e3fcef}
*_Roles of the parties (i.e. Bolt and Drivers)_*
{panel}

To my understanding, we won’t be instructing the Drivers to install CCTV cameras (i.e. it won’t be an obligation for them/a condition to continue providing services with Bolt).

We should provide guidelines to drivers stating that the installation of CCTV is entirely optional and not a condition for using the Bolt platform. This should clarify that Bolt is not instructing, mandating, or controlling this decision.

In my view, this doesn’t have to be overly formalized and can be managed by choosing the right words in our promotional material.

{panel:bgColor=#fffae6}
*_Ensuring privacy rights of Riders_*
{panel}

As independent controllers, drivers are solely responsible for complying with GDPR principles, such as lawfulness, fairness, transparency, and respecting data subject rights (riders).

Drivers must independently handle any data subject access requests or other rights exercised by Riders concerning the CCTV footage. In theory, however, I would assume that all of the requests will be channeled to Bolt (as there is no real possibility for the Rider to contact the Driver 24hours after the trip was finished). That’s why we have to be prepared to handle it on CS side.

I would like to loop in [~accountid:641d5e830e6828ab20269e6d] - we will be receiving DSRs here, therefore, we need to establish a process of responding to the Riders. *Important* - Bolt won’t have the recordings, therefore, we need to decide how we want to act on this: 1) refuse and say that Bolt has nothing to do with the CCTV initiative or 2) direct the Rider to the Driver. I do believe that the 2nd option is better legally speaking, but we would need to figure out how solve the question of sharing Driver’s data with Riders.

{panel:bgColor=#fffae6}
*_Drivers' compliance with applicable data protection laws_*
{panel}

I saw that as part of a selection process, the partners will have to demonstrate compliance with applicable laws, including [+ICO requirements+|https://ico.org.uk/for-organisations/uk-gdpr-guidance-and-resources/cctv-and-video-surveillance] (data controller & data processor arrangements, process in place for data handling, retention and disclosure, etc.).

* How will we ensure this? Will we only accept those Drivers who check all the boxes re ICO requirements?
* Will informing riders (i.e. having stickers in cars) be a prerequisite of having CCTV?
* Will we inform the drivers that it’s their responsibility to respond to riders re their privacy rights? What I mean by this one - I don’t think that Bolt will be able to respond to Riders, because we have no access to CCTV. We need to establish a process to manage user requests, because they have a right to receive a copy of the recording / right to request deletion.

----

To sum up, I see this activity as *medium risk* for Bolt, if we take all the necessary precautions to make sure 1) drivers act independently and 2) at the same time, drivers act in line with applicable laws.

*What is the biggest concern and is most likely to materialize* - Riders could complain about being monitored and will contact Bolt about this. I am afraid that we might receive regulatory investigations because *Riders will misunderstand that Bolt is responsible for CCTV*. So, to not let this risk materialize, we have to prepare in advance - make sure that Drivers take care of informing the riders.

