It seems that there could be more cases, where using an object _after_ running a {{CleaningObjectValidator}} on it could lead to unexpected results.

Consider the following snippet:

{noformat}const request = {
    mandatory: "field",
    nested: {
        value: "whatever"
    }
};

await this.tmpExternalApi.getTmp(ctx, request);

logger.info(ctx, "Whatever", {nested_value: request.nested.value});{noformat}

If the interface for external API does not contain the {{nested}} field, it will be deleted in line {{8}} and then line {{10}} will throw, despite the fact that the code compiles under {{strictNullChecks}} and according to the type-check, the {{nested}} field should be there.

Same can happen when using an object _after_ publishing it to the MQ and accessing an object in {{FireAndForget}} after it’s been returned in an endpoint’s response.

So, I’d suggest not to focus solely on the scenario described in the ticket, but rather to consider a general problem where modifying an object in the validator leads to unexpected behaviour when the object is used later.

